# {{project_name}} — Execution protocol ({{initiative_title}})
> **Protocol:** adcm-toolkits 0.15.2

> **What this is.** The operating manual of the build. It defines HOW the catalog in
> `detailed-plan.md` gets executed: **wave sessions** (not 1 task = 1 session),
> self-verified loops with the **DoD-auto as the exit criterion**, workflow fan-out
> where parallelism helps, **one code-simplifier pass over every wave's integrated
> diff before the final DoD run**, and **mandatory adversarial verification at
> ⚠gates**. The
> copy-paste prompts per wave are at the end (§7). Keep in sync with `task.md`
> (state) and `master-plan.md` (rationale). Prompts are tuned for Fable at high
> effort but run on any model.

## 1. Principles

1. **Session = one wave: 1–4 coupled tasks, one branch/MR per wave.**
2. **The loop ends by DoD, not by fatigue.** Each wave defines its **DoD-auto**: a
   list 100% verifiable by command. While it isn't green the loop continues:
   implement → verify → fix. Max 5 rounds; if the SAME failure doesn't converge in
   **3 attempts** → stop, report, **never degrade tests to pass**. Then ONE simplify
   pass (§1.11) → re-verify: the FULL DoD-auto runs AGAIN after it, so the green DoD
   is always the DoD of the shipped diff.
3. **DoD-auto ≠ DoD-human.** Actions only {{owner}} can take (dashboards, DNS,
   purchases, approvals) live in the **DoD-human**: when the DoD-auto is exhausted
   the task is marked `blocked` with the human checklist rendered at close. It only
   becomes `completed` when {{owner}} confirms.
4. **Implementers never self-approve at gates.** On ⚠gate waves the executor is an
   `adcm-toolkits:executor` called with `model: opus` and the loop does NOT close without **adversarial
   verification** by ANOTHER agent, an `adcm-toolkits:auditor` (the reserved one — never the
   executor itself, never the orchestrator's tier) that did NOT see the
   implementation, given only the diff + the DoD + that wave's attack checklist (§6). Its
   brief carries `REPORT PATH: {{docs_dir}}/qa/gate-<w>.md` — or the brain's existing layout (e.g. `qa/<wave>/gate-*.md` when `qa/` already has per-wave folders; never change an existing brain's convention): it returns the full report under
   `REPORT:` and the orchestrator saves it verbatim (a mechanical save, not an edit).
   This covers the simplifier too: it edits code, so it is an implementer — the
   orchestrator re-runs the DoD over its edits, and on ⚠gate waves the simplifier is
   neither the executor nor the reserved verifier, and runs BEFORE the verification.
5. **Inviolable decisions** (no session may contradict them — from
   `master-plan.md` §8): {{list_them_inline}}.
6. **No hardcoded dates**: the logbook uses the date of the day of execution.
7. **Agent budget 20 with fixed roles.** The main session (Fable) ORCHESTRATES:
   it decides design and scope, audits the deliverables `opus` investigators return
   (solution · files · considerations · open questions), writes one executor brief
   per task, runs every DoD-slice and the full DoD-auto itself, and integrates —
   it never implements first and never spawns its own tier. `opus` agents
   investigate (`adcm-toolkits:researcher`, called with `model: opus`), review regression
   and verify (`adcm-toolkits:auditor`) (quota 10 per session, 2 reserved — the
   ⚠gate verifier and the simplify pass; audits pass to `sonnet` once the rest is
   spent), and the ⚠gate executor is `adcm-toolkits:executor` + `model: opus`.
   `sonnet` agents (`adcm-toolkits:executor`; `adcm-toolkits:executor-frontend`
   when the wave's *Skills to load* are design skills) implement NO-gate tasks from briefs, ≤4 in
   parallel with disjoint files in their own worktrees (a shared helper is not a
   disjoint file: the task that extracts it closes before its consumers start), and
   never self-approve. ONE packaged `code-simplifier:code-simplifier` pass per wave,
   called with an explicit `model: opus` (one tier down with the roles when the main
   session is not Fable), costs 1 of the 20 and 1 of the Opus quota and is verified by
   the orchestrator, never by itself. One delivery `adcm-toolkits:courier` per batch (normally 1; each
   extra batch = 1 more of the 20, 0 Opus quota) — `sonnet` ALWAYS, even when the main
   session is not Fable — costs 1 of the 20 (the first is reserved, like the gate
   verifier) and 0 Opus quota (§2b step 7).
   **Escalation ladder** Sonnet → Opus → orchestrator: the 2nd failure of the SAME
   DoD line moves the task one tier up as a new agent carrying the attempt log.
   One counter per session, `model` explicit on every call or an `adcm-toolkits:*` type
   (a per-call `model` overrides the type's default: `researcher` + `model: opus` for Opus investigations, `executor` + `model: opus` for ⚠gate waves and Opus-written sections, `auditor` + `model: sonnet` once the Opus quota is spent or when the main session is one tier down; types fix the TOOL SET, the tier rules still set the model), `SendMessage` to a live agent costs 0. Ultracode changes the orchestration (Workflow per stage + adversarial
   cross-check), never roles or count. Exceeding 20 is a protocol violation; the
   logbook records `agents used: n/20 (opus a · sonnet b)` with each agent's role
   (investigate / execute / review / simplify / verify / capture / courier) and every escalation.
   Other packaged agents still name their `model`: the plugin's frontmatter is
   irrelevant. The role agents load at the next session or `/reload-plugins`; no plugin in
   this session → `Agent(subagent_type: "general-purpose", model: "<tier>")` with the same
   brief. On the orchestrator's own last-rung fixes (≤~20 lines,
   when a brief would cost more than the change): every one is LOGGED in the
   `task.md` logbook (`orchestrator fix: <file> — <why a brief cost more>`); an
   unlogged orchestrator edit is a protocol violation, not a shortcut. Saving a sub-agent's
   RETURN verbatim to the path its brief names (gate reports `qa/gate-<w>.md` or the brain's existing `qa/<wave>/gate-*.md` layout, digests) is a
   mechanical save, not an edit. The orchestrator
   is otherwise PURE: it never reads files in bulk, never edits at scale, never renders,
   browses or publishes, and every `Agent`/`Workflow` call names its `model` (Plan and
   Explore agents included) or is an `adcm-toolkits:*` type. The user's phrase `sin tanto lío` (that one task) or
   `modo directo` (until `modo orquestador`) switches this off and the main session
   does the work itself — rule and tier table in the skill's
   `references/orchestrator-rule.md`.
8. **Executor brief.** What the orchestrator hands to an executor, ≤40 lines:
   `TASK · FILES (from the Scope manifest — the shared helper and EVERY consumer to
   rewire included when the manifest says so) · SKILLS (the wave's Skills to load —
   the executor loads them with `Skill` before starting) · DELIVERABLE (Opus's audited deliverable
   with the orchestrator's decisions) · DESIGN DECISIONS (incl. the reuse verdict and
   the helper's home — not negotiable) · DOD-SLICE (command →
   expected; an extraction adds every rewired consumer's build/test and the duplication
   census → 1) · STOP IF (stop and report instead of improvising — including a consumer
   the census does not name, a helper home crossing repos, or duplication the brief
   does not name) · FORBIDDEN (paths outside the brief — the named helper and its
   consumers ARE in scope —, dependency changes, and comments narrating decisions,
   rationale or plan references, §1.10) ·
   RETURN (diff summary per file · commands + output · what was extracted or consumed
   and which consumers were rewired · open questions)`. Every §7
   prompt carries one pre-filled block per task, plus the wave's `## Simplifier brief`
   (§1.11). A poor brief produces poor code.

9. **REUSE before writing — decided in planning.** Logic that already exists is
   consumed; logic two or more components use or need is extracted ONCE into a shared
   helper both consume (rule of TWO, guarded by the same-behavior test: extract only
   when both consumers would change together — otherwise it is a deliberate duplicate
   with its why recorded). The verdict travels in the task's Scope manifest and in the
   prompt's `# SCOPE`; an executing session APPLIES it and runs the duplication census
   FIRST, it never hunts for duplication. The helper plus the rewiring of ALL its
   current consumers is ONE task — every consuming task lists it under `Depends on`, so
   it closes before they start and is never parallelised with them — and its DoD covers
   every rewired consumer. Duplication found anyway is reported, not improvised on: if
   every implicated file is already in this wave's SCOPE the orchestrator amends the
   briefs and extracts in-wave (helper first, consumers after, serialized); if any file
   is outside, it goes to the logbook `Reuse:` field as a follow-up candidate and into
   the later-wave manifests at doc-sync (§2b step 3).
10. **COMMENTS explain the WHY, never the plan.** Allowed: the why of non-obvious
    logic (an invariant, a workaround for a named external bug, a gotcha, a
    non-obvious algorithm), public-API docstrings in this repo's existing convention,
    TODOs pointing at the repo's issue tracker when it has one. Not allowed: restating
    what the code does, decision or rationale narration ("we chose X because"), change
    narration ("added in wave 2", "moved from"), plan references (task IDs, wave
    numbers, execution-doc names) and commented-out code. The reason: decisions already
    live in `master-plan.md` (§1 with its rejected alternatives, §3, §4, §8), in the
    `detailed-plan.md` cards and in the `task.md` logbook — a comment repeating them is
    a second record that rots. One Base DoD line makes the plan-reference half
    command-verifiable; narration vs why is a judgement call and belongs to the `opus`
    regression review and to the simplifier, never to a fuzzy grep.
11. **SIMPLIFY — one pass per wave.** Once the regression findings are fixed, the
    orchestrator makes a checkpoint commit and runs the packaged agent
    `code-simplifier:code-simplifier` with an explicit `model: opus` over the wave's
    integrated diff, driven by the `## Simplifier brief` (≤20 lines: the diff's file
    list and nothing else · the project conventions override the agent's built-in style
    defaults · the executor briefs' design decisions are not negotiable · the comment
    policy of §1.10 · no behavior, dependency, formatting-only or test changes ·
    returns a diff summary per file and what it skipped). It costs 1 of the 20 and 1 of
    the Opus quota, never self-approves, and on ⚠gate waves runs BEFORE the reserved
    verifier. Cap: ONE pass, edits applied in ONE round, never a simplify → verify
    loop. If the FULL DoD-auto fails after it: ONE fix round to the SAME simplifier
    (`SendMessage`, cost 0 — if it cannot be reached, skip straight to the revert); a
    second failure goes back to the checkpoint commit (`git reset --hard` while the
    branch is unpushed, `git revert` of the simplify commit if already pushed) and the
    wave closes on the pre-simplify diff, recorded in the logbook. That retry consumes
    neither the 5 rounds nor the 3 attempts. The simplifier edits the wave branch in
    place; the orchestrator commits its diff. The brief is the contract and the packaged
    agent only its default carrier: where `code-simplifier:code-simplifier` is not
    installed, the same `## Simplifier brief` goes to ONE `adcm-toolkits:executor` with
    `model: <auditor tier>` (the auditor type has no Edit) — same model, cost, cap and RETURN — and the logbook `Simplifier:` field records which
    carrier ran.

