## 2b. Doc-sync at close (mandatory — "append the logbook" means ALL of this)

Closing a wave (DoD-auto green) — and every later status change (e.g. {{owner}}
merges a ⚠gate) — updates ALL plan docs in ONE pass, one Conventional commit:

1. **`task.md`** — logbook entry with the extended schema (date of execution ·
   branch/MR · tasks closed · **blocked** · **files touched** · **manifest drift** · notes · wall
   clock · **reuse** (helpers consumed/extracted · consumers rewired · dup census
   before → after · unplanned duplication found → follow-up | none) · **simplifier**
   (carrier: packaged | fallback sub-agent · ran over `{{checkpoint_sha}}..HEAD` · files
   touched · DoD re-verified | reverted (why) | not run (why)) · **agents used n/20 (opus a · sonnet b)** with roles investigate /
   execute / review / simplify / verify / capture / courier (record the NUMBER of couriers: 1, or more
   when the delivery ran in several batches) · **escalations** · **Next:** exact next
   command/prompt, or "wave closed") AND the
   wave-map status flip.
2. **`execute.md` §7** — this wave's prompt header flip (`☐` → `✅ DONE (date)` /
   `🔄` / `⛔`).
3. **`detailed-plan.md`** — mark the tasks done; update their Scope manifests with
   what was ACTUALLY touched; for every LATER wave whose manifest names surfaces this
   wave changed, refresh its file lists and census expected counts. A helper born in
   this wave is written into the `Reuse:` verdict and the Read-first list of every
   later wave that needs the same logic, so the next wave consumes it instead of
   duplicating it again.
4. **`execute.md` §7** — regenerate the prompt of any later wave whose manifest
   changed in (3): its `# SCOPE` must match detailed-plan.
5. **Delivery message — ENDS with the courier's links block, no filler.** The links
   come from the `artifact-courier` (step 7), never from the main session: its returned
   block holds (a) the wave's viewable result when it renders, ALWAYS AS TWO LINES —
   the `localhost` URL FIRST ({{owner}} validates from iTerm on the Mac) and the
   LAN-IP URL after it (so it also opens from a phone); never one without the other,
   and never the LAN IP alone: it changes with the network, so a link handed over
   yesterday arrives dead while `localhost` keeps working — and (b) ALWAYS the
   project's canonical artifacts from `{{docs_dir}}/artifacts.json` (plans, prompts,
   mockups…), even when this wave rendered nothing new. The orchestrator only starts
   the preview server if none runs (bound to `0.0.0.0`, with HTTP Range support or video
   is dead on iOS; same for a served PDF or report) and passes the localhost URL in the
   courier's brief; the courier reads the LAN IP (`ipconfig getifaddr en0`) and `curl`s
   both URLs before returning them.
   **Order of the final message**: repo paths and commit hashes (one line each —
   reference, not delivery) and the pending DoD-human lines go ABOVE the links block,
   never after it: the guard rejects any text after the last link. The block itself is
   the courier's `=== LINKS ===` tail pasted verbatim as the LAST lines — no headings,
   no text inside it.
   **Format — links must be TAPPABLE on a phone**: each link is a plain Markdown
   bullet `- [emoji Title](url)` — NEVER inside a code fence, inline backticks, or
   4-space indentation ({{owner}} reads closes on a phone, where code-formatted
   links render as dead text). The raw URL living inside the Markdown link also
   keeps Claude Code's quick-access footer badges (below the mode indicator)
   working — both surfaces, one format.
   **Media before the final message**: the courier returns the screenshots of the VISUAL
   CHECK (a GIF when the feature spans several screens) as `MEDIA: unsent: <paths>` and
   you send them with SendUserFile (sub-agents do not have that tool); the
   narrative comes first, then the files, then a SHORT final message — the pending
   DoD-human lines and then the block — so on a phone {{owner}} sees the media and
   links without scrolling back up; nothing follows the block.
   The `artifact-guard` Stop hook blocks the close when the block is missing.
6. **Project context skill — DELTA refresh**: with the logbook's files-touched list,
   update only the affected references of `{{project_context_skill}}` (usage-map
   consumer counts, folder inventories, entry hints if new surfaces were born) and
   bump their `last_scanned`. Use the generator's incremental mode
   (`scan_project.py --update <paths>`) when available. Every closed wave leaves the
   brain AND the code map fresh — freshness never depends on manual re-scans.
7. **`master-plan.md` execution-state block, `plans.html` and `prompts.html` —
   REGENERATE AND REPUBLISH, by the courier**: flip the row/badge in master-plan's
   execution-state block and finish the doc-sync of steps 1–4 and 9 — commit and push
   it FIRST (a project pre-commit may regenerate HTML when it commits the `.md`). The
   main session never opens, renders or edits brain HTML and never calls the Artifact
   tool: it delegates the delivery to one `adcm-toolkits:courier` per batch (it preloads the skill
   `adcm-toolkits:artifact-courier`; brief from its `templates/courier-brief.md`,
   `sonnet` fixed; normally 1 courier, each extra batch = 1 more of the 20, 0 Opus quota). The courier runs each registry
   row's `regen` (`plans-regen.py` for `plans.html`, `prompts-regen.py` for
   `prompts.html`, both copied with their `.tmpl` to `{{docs_dir}}/scripts/`), republishes
   every stale artifact to its SAME URL — or re-issues it as a new one above ~600 KB —
   stamps `published_at`/`version`/`sha256` in `{{docs_dir}}/artifacts.json`, commits
   that file, returns the media paths (`MEDIA: unsent`) and a table plus the links block. `prompts.html`
   changes whenever a §7 header flips or a prompt is regenerated, so BOTH pages are
   republished at practically every close; its only source of truth is the
   `### Wave <ID>` headers in §7. A badge flipped on disk without a republish leaves
   the shared link stale (lesson learned: a wave closed with the published plan still
   showing "pending"). New artifact ⇒ new row in `artifacts.json` (without `url`) in
   the same session: the courier publishes it. If the courier's RETURN carries
   `ERRORS`, send ONE `SendMessage` to the SAME courier (cost 0) and report; never
   publish yourself. The `artifact-guard` Stop hook blocks the close while a registered
   artifact is stale — it trusts the courier's stamps.
8. {{If this plan family coexists with an INCUMBENT protocol (the project already
   keeps its own live TASKS/EXECUTION docs): state explicitly which docs THIS family
   owns and which belong to the incumbent — never write to the incumbent's docs from
   these prompts; report the pending incumbent update to {{owner}} instead.}}
9. **COMMIT AND PUSH the docs repo — the doc-sync does not exist until pushed.**
   `{{docs_dir}}` is its own NESTED git repo inside the container; the harness /
   environment header reports the working directory as "not a git repo" — that is the
   CONTAINER, don't believe it: verify with `git -C {{docs_dir}} status`. Close with
   `git -C {{docs_dir}} add -A && git commit -m "docs({{wave}}): …" && git push`. An
   unpushed logbook is invisible to the next session and to {{owner}} from another
   machine (lesson learned: a whole wave's docs once sat uncommitted for days).

⚠gate nuance: at session exit the wave is "verified, pending {{owner}}", not ✅;
re-run this doc-sync when {{owner}} pushes/merges.

## 3. Delivery / merge policy

| Wave type | Delivery |
|---|---|
| NO-gate | {{e.g. auto-merge the MR if pipeline green and DoD-auto passed / commit without push for review}} — only once the simplify pass ran and the DoD-auto was re-run green AFTER it |
| ⚠gate ({{gate_wave_ids}}) | **No merge** — {{owner}} reviews after the simplify pass and the adversarial verification |

Nothing is delivered — MR opened, merged, or committed for review — on a diff the
simplify pass has not seen, or on a DoD that predates it (§1.11).

## 4. Checkpoint / resume

If a session dies mid-wave: partial state lives in (a) the wave branch's checkpoint
commits and (b) a `[PARTIAL]` logbook entry with: internal tasks closed, exact step in
progress, which executor tier held each open task and on which rung of the ladder
(attempt log), unmerged worktrees, whether the simplify pass already ran and over
which checkpoint commit (so the resuming session neither repeats nor skips it), and
its `**Next:**` line (the next command to run). The resuming session
runs `status-digest --brain {{docs_dir}}` (LAST LOG gives the
`[PARTIAL]` entry's next/blocked/agents and its `@La-b` range; `--entry K` — K from LAST
LOG/prev — prints it in full), checks out the branch and continues the loop from that
rung — it does NOT restart, and it does NOT re-brief tasks whose worktree is intact.

